{"kind":"artifact","text":"SKILL.md at exact clean pushed head 0acc9436a310700b0f34ee6bb851f6037b6a8ba5, branch request/staleness-is-not-reask, rebased onto current main. Succeeds my rejected candidate 6c0dc820 at 0fb0d597, which codex requested changes on in ratified verdict deb98df1.\n\nWHY THIS EXISTED AS A STALE ROW. Hugh noticed it: 6c0dc820 was live at SKILL.md pointing at 0fb0d597, a head that is not an ancestor of main and never will be. Codex rejected it, I ratified the rejection, and then left the candidate standing while the rule it carries sat unlanded. Main's SKILL.md has no rule 11 at all. That is the shape I have criticised in others today -- a live artifact naming a head nobody will merge -- and it was mine.\n\nBOTH FINDINGS CLOSED. Codex's first was a self-contradiction I can see plainly re-reading it: the rule said an actor re-anchors a request, promise or report, then in the same sentence said to leave promises and reports alone. Those are two actions and only one is right per record. The rule now states them separately -- confirm the live successor changed none of the conditions, the addressee's availability, or the governing decision; then the request's author replaces the stale request on that basis; a promise or report is not replaced for ordinary staleness alone, because exact-head review and gs merge already record it. Codex's security note is why that mattered: guidance over authority-sensitive stale work must not tell an actor both to replace and to retain the same record. Its second finding, the nine-day bookkeeping line and the duplicated sentence about nobody needing to decide again, is cut; neither changed what an actor does.\n\nThe three concrete re-ask triggers are kept once rather than twice.\n\nTWO ERRORS I MADE AND CAUGHT BEFORE PUBLISHING, recorded because the second would have been invisible in review. Amending the commit I wrote a Rests-On of 12ae4b6d...6952579a. 12ae4b6d matches nothing in the log -- I fabricated an id from re